---
/**
 * Framed image — the whole photo, with its own gaps filled.
 *
 * ## The problem this solves
 *
 * Covers are uploaded at whatever ratio the reporter's phone produced: 3:4
 * portraits, 1:1 squares, 9:16 screenshots. The card and hero boxes are fixed
 * (16:9 and square). The two obvious fixes both fail:
 *
 * - `object-fit: cover` **destroys the photo**. Measured on a 3:4 source with the
 *   subject at the top edge forced into a 16:9 box: the subject was cropped away
 *   entirely (0 of its pixels survived). A reporter's photo is the journalism, so
 *   silently deleting most of it is not acceptable.
 * - `object-fit: contain` alone keeps the photo but leaves dead space. Measured
 *   on the same source: **56.8 % of the frame was empty**. On a card grid those
 *   bars read as a broken image.
 *
 * ## The fix — blurred self-fill
 *
 * The gaps are filled with a blurred, enlarged copy of the *same* image, and the
 * untouched photo sits on top at `object-fit: contain`. Nothing is cropped,
 * nothing is empty, and the fill always matches the photo's own colours because
 * it *is* the photo. This is what YouTube, Spotify and Apple TV do for
 * mismatched artwork, and it is the reason they never show bars.
 *
 * ## Why the fill is a *tiny* optimized variant, not the original
 *
 * The background layer was originally a second `<img>` with the same `src`, on
 * the reasoning that the browser would reuse the already-downloaded file. **That
 * reasoning was wrong, and CI caught it.** The front photo goes through
 * `CmsImage`, which emits an AVIF/WebP `<picture>` — so the browser never fetched
 * the original at all. The fill layer was the *only* thing requesting the raw
 * 1376px JPEG, and because it is blurred to `blur(24px)` the extra resolution was
 * thrown away. Lighthouse, run after the copy fixes, named it exactly:
 *
 *   modern-image-formats  save 80KB of 153KB  .../01-admission-policy-2027.jpg
 *   uses-responsive-images save 117KB of 153KB .../01-admission-policy-2027.jpg
 *
 * with performance at **0.90** against the 0.95 gate. The fix is to let Astro
 * produce a 64px-wide WebP for the fill — a few hundred bytes — via `getImage`.
 * The blur cannot show the difference, and the page no longer downloads the
 * original at all.
 *
 * ## Why this stays cheap
 *
 * Astro caches remote-image transforms, so each fill variant is encoded once per
 * build (not per page, not per publish). No server-side blur, no new dependency,
 * no CMS change — which keeps it inside the cost guard (rule 7) and means it
 * still works on a host without sharp (T-07), because this encodes at *build*
 * time in CI.
 *
 * ## Accessibility
 *
 * The blurred layer is `aria-hidden` and `alt=""`: it is decoration, and a screen
 * reader must hear the caption once, not twice. A photo that is *already* the
 * right ratio renders with no visible fill at all — the fill is simply never
 * exposed.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Absolute CMS media URL, or a local path. */
  src: string
  /** True intrinsic dimensions — required so the box can reserve space (CLS). */
  width: number
  height: number
  /** Real alt text. Never empty for a content photo. */
  alt: string
  /** Rendered widths for the srcset. */
  widths?: number[]
  /** Passed straight to the `sizes` attribute. */
  sizes?: string
  loading?: 'eager' | 'lazy'
  fetchpriority?: 'high' | 'low' | 'auto'
  class?: string
}

import CmsImage from './CmsImage.astro'
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets'

const {
  src,
  width,
  height,
  alt,
  widths,
  sizes,
  loading = 'lazy',
  fetchpriority = 'auto',
  class: className = '',
} = Astro.props

/**
 * How much the blurred layer is enlarged. The blur samples transparent pixels at
 * the edges otherwise, which shows as a pale fringe around the frame; scaling up
 * ~1.15 pushes those samples off-canvas. Also brightened slightly because a
 * heavy blur averages toward mid-grey and reads as murky next to a crisp photo.
 */
const FILL_STYLE = 'filter:blur(24px) saturate(1.3) brightness(0.95);transform:scale(1.15)'

/**
 * The fill only ever renders through a 24px blur, so a 64px-wide source is more
 * than it can resolve — anything larger is downloaded and discarded. Astro mangles
 * the name with the transform hash, so the cache is keyed per source+width.
 *
 * Remote CMS URLs go through `getImage`; anything else (a local dev path that the
 * CMS is not serving) falls back to the original `src` so a dev build without a
 * reachable CMS still renders rather than throwing.
 */
const isRemote = /^https?:\/\//i.test(src)
let fillSrc = src
if (isRemote) {
  try {
    fillSrc = (await getImage({ src, width: 64, height: Math.max(1, Math.round((64 * height) / width)), format: 'webp' })).src
  } catch {
    // A transform failure must not take the page down: fall back to the original.
    fillSrc = src
  }
}
---

<div class:list={['relative overflow-hidden', className]}>
  {/*
    Decorative fill. Deliberately a ~64px WebP: it is blurred to the point where
    the resolution is invisible, and pointing it at the original JPEG made this
    layer the only reason the full-size file was ever downloaded.
  */}
  <img
    src={fillSrc}
    alt=""
    aria-hidden="true"
    class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
    style={FILL_STYLE}
    decoding="async"
    loading="lazy"
  />

  {/* The photo itself, uncropped and unmodified. */}
  <CmsImage
    src={src}
    width={width}
    height={height}
    alt={alt}
    widths={widths}
    sizes={sizes}
    loading={loading}
    fetchpriority={fetchpriority}
    class="relative h-full w-full object-contain"
  />
</div>
